<!-- Global CSS -->
{/* ── the stylesheet carries the build that made it ─────────────────
    /styles/global.css lives in public/, so it is copied through
    untouched and its URL never changes. Astro hashes everything it
    bundles for exactly this reason; this file opts out of that, and
    then behaves exactly as you would expect something with a permanent
    URL and changing contents to behave.

    It bit twice in one day. The service worker served it
    stale-while-revalidate, so a deploy only appeared on the second
    load. And the CDN held its own copy, so the site served new HTML —
    with markup that did not exist a day earlier — against a stylesheet
    from before any of it was written. A drawer built by one build,
    styled by another. That is not a layout bug to chase; it is two
    files from different days.

    The stamp makes the URL change whenever the build does, so no
    cache anywhere — browser, service worker or edge — can hand back
    yesterday's styles for today's markup. The commit SHA on Vercel,
    the build time everywhere else.
    ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */}
<link rel="stylesheet" href={`/styles/global.css?v=${cssVersion}`} />

{/* The Swiper stylesheet used to load here from jsdelivr on every page.
    Nothing on the site uses Swiper — it was a third-party request for
    nothing, on the critical path of every visit. */}

